  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["J7", "J8"],
      "pins": ["J7.1", "J7.2", "J7.3", "J8.1", "J8.2", "J8.3"],
      "nets": ["+12V", "FAN_0_TACH", "FAN_1_TACH", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet (page 7, column 5) the +12V arrow feeds a vertical wire that taps TWO pins of each fan header (the top pin and the third pin), the FAN_0_TACH / FAN_1_TACH labels are tied to the second pin, and the fourth pin is a no-connect; no GND symbol is connected to either fan header. Thus +12V appears on two header pins and the fan ground pin is left unconnected.",
      "expected": "The specification traces the +12V net as containing exactly one fan-header pin per connector: 'the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)'. A 4-pin fan header must carry +12V on pin 2, GND on pin 1 and the tach signal on pin 3, with the tach net on a different pin than +12V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Also on this net: U5 pin 7/7_1 (IN1), D7 (plain diode, Device:D), bulk caps C1 (value not separately captured) and C2, and the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet, right block: '+12V' arrow joined by a green wire with two junction dots to two stubs of J7 (and J8); red label 'FAN_0_TACH'/'FAN_1_TACH' on the remaining stub; no GND symbol on the headers."}
      ],
      "impact": "A plugged fan receives +12V on what should be its GND pin and the board's tach input (a 3.3V-pulled, TVS-clamped line) on its +12V pin, with no ground return. The fan will not spin, tach monitoring is lost, and 12V may be forced into the 3.3V tach/TVS network, potentially damaging D5/D6 and the ESP32 input.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic matches the specification on every value, part and most connections (power mux, buck, SPI/CS GPIO map, USB-C CC resistors, heater switch, thermocouple channels, WS2812), but the fan headers J7/J8 are mis-wired: +12V is tied to two header pins with the tach signal on pin 2 and no GND pin, contradicting the spec's netlist trace that puts +12V only on pin 2 and requires a fan ground."
}

